{#
  THE ACCESSIBILITY STATEMENT. Rewritten 2026-08-18, replacing the text
  transcribed from the previous site on 2026-08-01.

  WHY IT WAS REPLACED RATHER THAN EDITED. The migrated version described
  UserWay's widget and scanner, and this build ships no overlay: CLAUDE.md bans
  them on the grounds that they raise legal risk rather than lower it. Worse
  than merely stale, it told a visitor to click an accessibility menu icon "that
  appears on the corner of the page", which is an instruction to a control that
  does not exist, on the one page a person who has hit a barrier is most likely
  to open. A statement that misdirects the reader it exists to help is worse
  than no statement.

  It also carried the migrated-content banner, which stops being true the moment
  the text is ours, so that is gone too.

  FOUR THINGS AND NOTHING MORE: the standard targeted, an honest account of
  conformance including the gaps, how to report a barrier, and what we will do
  about one. The migrated version's General and Disclaimer sections said nothing
  a reader could act on, and the Disclaimer used a word on the house banned list.

  ============================================================
  EVERY CLAIM BELOW IS SUBSTANTIATED IN THE REPO. Checked 2026-08-18.
  ============================================================

    contrast measured        tools/verify/contrast.mjs, the declared token
                             matrix plus a live scan of every text-carrying
                             element on the built pages
    focus indicator          HANDOFF.md, measured on paper, mist and white when
                             --focus-ring moved from .45 to .70
    skip link                base.njk, first element in the body
    reduced motion           prefers-reduced-motion in 10 files under src/css/
    explicit labels          contact.njk, for/id rather than wrapping
    errors not color alone   form.css, aria-invalid plus a text message in a
                             live region, added when the form was wired

  THREE CLAIMS FROM THE APPROVED DRAFT WERE CUT FOR BEING UNTRUE OR UNTESTED,
  and this note exists so nobody restores them from that draft:

  1. "checked on every build, and the build fails". FALSE. contrast.mjs runs
     under `npm run verify`, and Cloudflare's build command is `npm run build`.
     A contrast regression does not fail a deploy. The text now says it runs
     before a release, which is what actually happens.
  2. "every page is keyboard reachable". UNTESTED. Nothing in the repo measures
     it. It is probably true and it is not ours to assert.
  3. A single figure for the focus ratio on paper. HANDOFF.md records 4.19:1 in
     its measurement table and 4.23:1 in its audit answer. Mist is 3.95:1 in
     both, and mist is the constraint, so the text states the floor that holds
     under either reading.
#}

{% call container(class="cs-doc") %}
  <h1 class="cs-doc__title">Accessibility Statement</h1>
  <p class="cs-doc__date">Last reviewed {{ reviewed | calendarDate }}</p>

  <p class="cs-doc__lede">
    Circulation Studio targets WCAG 2.1 Level AA for this site. We have not
    commissioned an independent audit and we do not claim full conformance.
    What we can state is what we have measured.
  </p>

  <div class="cs-doc__body">
    <h2>What we have measured</h2>
    <p>Color contrast is checked automatically, both against every text and surface pairing the design system permits and against every text-carrying element on the built pages. A pairing below the AA threshold fails that check.</p>
    <p>Every page opens with a skip link. The focus indicator measures 3.95:1 or better on every ground it appears on, where the standard asks for 3:1. Reduced-motion preferences are honored throughout. Form controls carry explicit labels, and form errors are announced to assistive technology and stated in words rather than signaled by color alone.</p>

    <h2>What we have not done</h2>
    <p>There has been no independent audit. We have not tested with screen readers across assistive technologies, we have not audited reflow at 320 pixels, and we do not run an automated accessibility scan over the whole site. The contrast check above runs before a release rather than on every deploy.</p>
    <p>We would rather list these than imply they are covered. Where any of them turns up a failure, it will be described here.</p>

    <h2>Reporting a barrier</h2>
    <p>If any part of this site is difficult or impossible for you to use, please tell us. You do not need to identify the standard involved or describe the problem in technical terms. What you were trying to do, and what happened instead, is enough.</p>
    {#- A plain mailto and a plain tel, deliberately, and not the obfuscated
        email macro the contact page uses. That macro assembles the address in
        JavaScript to keep a harvestable string out of the markup, which is the
        right trade on a marketing page and the wrong one here: this is the
        route for somebody who has already hit a barrier, and it must not depend
        on JavaScript running or on an address that a screen reader reaches only
        after a script has assembled it. -#}
    <p class="cs-doc__contact">
      {{ site.nap.name }}<br>
      <a href="{{ site.nap.telephoneHref }}">{{ site.nap.telephone }}</a><br>
      <a href="mailto:{{ site.nap.email }}">{{ site.nap.email }}</a>
    </p>

    <h2>What we will do</h2>
    <p>We will respond within five business days. Within thirty days of that response we will either have fixed the barrier or given you a dated plan for fixing it.</p>
    <p>We do not promise a fix inside a fixed window for a problem nobody has seen yet. Some barriers are a one-line change and some are a rebuild of a component, and a deadline set before the work is understood is a deadline that gets quietly missed. A dated plan is a commitment we can keep.</p>
  </div>
{% endcall %}
